# The listener that asks for a client certificate, imported by CADDY_EXTRA_CONFIG when a deployment
# serves the mTLS endpoints of RFC 8705 ("import mtls.Caddyfile", as worker.Caddyfile is imported).
# MTLS_SERVER_NAME names the host it serves, which is the one "mtls_endpoint_aliases" will point a client
# at; the ordinary host of SERVER_NAME is served by the Caddyfile beside this file and keeps asking for
# no certificate at all. Two hosts and one listener: a browser, the conformance suite and oidc-demo go on
# reaching the ordinary one and are never asked for anything.
#
# "mode request" asks the client for a certificate and serves the connection when it presents none
# (Caddy 2.11), which is what a provider needs: a client that authenticates another way must not fail at
# the handshake, and "self_signed_tls_client_auth" (RFC 8705 §2.2) presents a certificate that chains
# nowhere. The handshake still proves the client holds the private key of what it presented, since a
# certificate is only accepted with its CertificateVerify signature (RFC 8446 §4.4.3). What it does not
# prove is a chain: a deployment doing "tls_client_auth" (§2.1) adds a "trust_pool" here, because only
# the leaf reaches PHP and no chain can be validated there (docs/mtls-at-the-edge.md).
#
# The leaf is handed to PHP in a variable and not in a header: a client sets every header of its request
# and no variable of the process that serves it. Caddy replaces the placeholder per request, and fills
# the envelope with nothing when the client presented nothing.
#
# "fapi_ciphers" is the snippet of the Caddyfile beside this file: both hosts offer the cipher suites
# FAPI 1.0 Advanced Final §8.5 permits, and this one is the host a FAPI run calls the token endpoint on.
{$MTLS_SERVER_NAME} {
	log {
		{$CADDY_SERVER_LOG_OPTIONS}
	}

	tls {
		import fapi_ciphers

		client_auth {
			mode request
		}
	}

	root * /app/public
	encode zstd br gzip

	php_server {
		env SSL_CLIENT_CERT {http.request.tls.client.certificate_pem}
	}
}
